Here's one indicator of where things are in our country right now.
这里有一个指标，能说明我们国家目前的情况。

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.040
Now, housing prices are rising.
现在，房价正在上涨。

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.900
More homes are being listed for sale, which is a sign of health, of course.
挂牌出售的房屋更多了，这当然是一个健康的迹象。

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.090
That is from a report issued this week from Realtor.com.
这是 Realtor.com 本周发布的一份报告中的内容。

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:15.700
Cities like Houston and Sacramento have housing markets that are described as red hot.
休斯顿和萨克拉门托等城市的住房市场被形容为炙手可热。

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.340
The Quad Cities are improving.
双城地区（Quad Cities）正在改善。

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:21.500
And in the Twin Cities, housing prices are up over 15% of where they were a year ago.
而在双城（Twin Cities），房价比一年前上涨了15%以上。

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:29.960
There's enough demand that on Monday, real estate agent Sophia Tufam went from door to door in a suburb of St. Paul asking people if they would consider selling their homes.
需求如此旺盛，以至于周一房地产经纪人索菲亚·图法姆在圣保罗的一个郊区挨家挨户敲门，询问人们是否考虑出售房屋。

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:39.140
I like to have a medium knock as if it were, you know, as if I was knocking on a friend's house.
我喜欢用中等力度的敲门声，就好像是，你知道的，就像我在敲朋友家的门一样。

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:42.260
I don't want to make it sound like I'm the cops at the door or anything like that.
我不想让它听起来像警察在敲门或类似的事情。

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:49.860
When a man answers the door, Sophia explains that she has a buyer who wants to purchase in the neighborhood and she is looking for somebody who might want to sell.
当一名男子开门时，索菲亚解释说，她有一个买家想在这个社区买房，她正在寻找可能想卖房的人。

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.720
Like most people on Monday, this guy was very responsive.
像周一的大多数人一样，这家伙非常配合。

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:57.560
We've considered moving to a house because we've got another baby on the way right now.
我们一直在考虑换个房子，因为我们现在正迎来另一个宝宝。

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.740
So we have four now, but we've got number five.
所以我们现在有四个孩子，但第五个快要...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:28.520
coming ah and so it's something we would think about I guess we could you know actually agree to a reasonable price but this is actually a very nice unit if you want to come in and take a look we've got four bedrooms in here and five bathrooms right so we have this bathroom here yes at this point the impromptu Victoria runs into the guy's pregnant wife, who's napping.
来了，啊，所以这是我们会考虑的事情，我想我们真的可以，你知道的，接受一个合理的价位，但如果你想进来看看的话，这其实是一个非常不错的单位，我们这里有四个卧室和五个卫生间，对吧，所以我们这里有这个卫生间，是的，在这个时候，即兴造访的维多利亚遇到了这家伙正在打瞌睡的怀孕妻子。

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:32.320
I'm just letting someone take a look inside.
我只是让别人进来看看。

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:33.800
We're not selling.
我们不卖。

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:35.200
We just moved here.
我们刚搬来。

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:42.140
Yeah, he was mentioning maybe there was a thought about moving into a single-family home, something larger.
是的，他提到过也许有考虑搬到单户住宅，大一点的地方。

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.020
Not that I know of.
我不知道。

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:47.540
I just moved in.
我才刚搬进来。

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:49.500
Phone numbers are exchanged.
交换了电话号码。

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.240
Polite maybe will be in touches.
客套地说了句“保持联系”。

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:52.800
And on to the next house.
然后走向下一家。

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:54.860
Commerce marches on.
商业在继续行进。

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:25.500
so turning to our first big story well fallout continued all this week from the revelations about the national security agency collecting data of the phone numbers that we dial and how long we talk and the location of our cell phones and how the nsa is also collecting and storing Throwing emails, file transfers, photos, videos, chats.
那么转到我们的第一个大新闻，嗯，本周关于国家安全局收集我们拨打电话号码的数据、通话时长、手机位置，以及国安局如何收集和存储电子邮件、文件传输、照片、视频、聊天的爆料引发的余波仍在继续。

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:27.220
You have probably heard all of that.
你可能已经听说过这一切了。

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:30.340
Government officials, of course, are saying that there's nothing to be alarmed about.
当然，政府官员表示没有什么好惊慌的。

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.180
Nobody is listening to our phone calls.
没有人听我们的电话。

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:37.080
Emails they're reading are those of non-citizens outside the U.S.
他们阅读的电子邮件是美国境外的非公民的邮件。

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:42.240
And I think that lots of us, including me, have the feeling of, okay, I'm boring.
我想我们很多人，包括我在内，都有这样一种感觉：好吧，我很无聊。

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:46.520
I don't care if the government knows what phone calls I make or reads my stupid emails.
我才不在乎政府是否知道我打了什么电话，或者阅读我那些愚蠢的电子邮件。

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:53.760
But of course, people like me and maybe you have no idea what we're talking about when it comes to being monitored by the government.
但当然，像我和你这样的人，对于被政府监控到底是怎么回事根本一无所知。

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:55.580
We have no idea of what that really means.
我们根本不知道那究竟意味着什么。

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:59.060
So I thought it might be helpful to talk to some people who do.
所以我想，和那些真正知道的人聊聊可能会有帮助。

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:04.980
And there is a group of Americans who know with reasonable certainty that they are being spied on by their own government.
有一群美国人有相当大的把握确信他们正在被自己的政府监视。

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:07.680
And that is the lawyers for Guantanamo detainees.
那就是关塔那摩关押人员的律师们。

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:14.080
For years now, it's been known that if you are talking to suspected terrorists, your communications are subject to surveillance.
多年来，众所周知，如果你在与疑似恐怖分子交谈，你的通讯就会受到监控。

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:18.080
Talking to suspected terrorists is basically these lawyers' job description.
而与疑似恐怖分子交谈基本上就是这些律师的工作职责。

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.720
And one of these attorneys, Candace Gorman, said that her phone started acting weird when she started taking Guantanamo cases in 2006.
其中一位律师坎迪斯·戈尔曼说，自从她在2006年开始接手关塔那摩的案子以来，她的电话就开始出现奇怪的状况。

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:29.600
When she would miss a call, two numbers would appear on her phone instead of one.
当她漏掉一个电话时，手机上会显示两个号码，而不是一个。

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:37.500
And I could actually call back the other call that was missed, and it would be picked up by what was called a special operator.
而且我实际上可以回拨那个未接的另一个电话，接听的是一个被称为特殊接线员的人。

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.920
And I brought my phone to a service.
我把手机送去维修。

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.000
I'm trying to think of what the guy was called.
我想想那个人叫什么来着。

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:50.960
And the first thing he did when he looked at it was said, do you have any reason to believe that your phone is being tapped?
他看了一眼后做的第一件事就是说：“你有什么理由相信你的电话被窃听了吗？”

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:53.280
And so what has that done to your communications?
那么这给你的通讯带来了什么影响？

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:56.180
Like, what do you do differently because of that, if anything?
比如，因为这个，你做了什么不同的事情，如果有的话？

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.140
Well, I stopped taking cases back in 2007.
嗯，我在2007年就停止接案子了。

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:13.680
If I was being monitored, which I believed I was, and I believe I still am, I knew I couldn't give confidentiality to my clients, which is one of the main things attorneys have to promise their clients.
如果我被监控，而我相信我确实被监控了，并且我相信我现在依然被监控着，我知道我无法对我的客户保持保密，这是律师必须向客户承诺的主要事情之一。

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:18.820
Several lawyers told me very believable stories about being monitored while talking about their cases.
几位律师向我讲述了非常可信的故事，关于他们在讨论案件时受到监控。

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:27.940
In February, you may have read, a government official admitted that there are microphones hidden in the rooms at Guantanamo where lawyers have their supposedly confidential conversations with their clients.
二月份的时候，你可能读到过，一名政府官员承认，在关塔那摩的房间里藏有麦克风，律师们在那里与客户进行理应保密的对话。

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:30.460
But of course, monitoring may go way beyond that.
但当然，监控可能远不止于此。

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:37.580
David Nevin represents the man who's called the architect of the 9-11 attacks, He attacks Khalil Sheikh Mohammed, and he represents other detainees as well.
大卫·内文代表着被称为“9·11袭击策划者”哈利德·谢赫·穆罕默德的这个人，他也代表其他被关押人员。

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:43.620
He says that there's no way to really know the extent that private, non-work conversations are being listened in on.
他说，私下的、与工作无关的对话被窃听的程度究竟有多深，根本无法真正知晓。

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:44.560
But still.
但即便如此。

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:49.340
If you're being listened in on, how do you talk to people?
如果你正在被窃听，你该怎么和别人说话？

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.400
How do you talk to the people who are closest to you?
你怎么和离你最近的人说话？

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:56.900
You say that silly, loving thing that you might have said.
你会说出那些可能会说的傻气的、充满爱意的话吗？

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.680
I mean, will you just keep certain private information off just because you just feel like, I don't want them to know that.
我的意思是，你会仅仅因为觉得“我不想让他们知道那个”而隐瞒某些私密信息吗？

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:28.470
that's that's not for them absolutely absolutely can you think of a time when you did that an example that isn't so personal that you can't say it on the radio so I'm asking you to zero in on something that's too personal for government eavesdroppers but not so personal you don't mind saying it to to, like, two and a half million people.
那，那不是给他们看的，绝对，绝对，你能想到你曾经这样做过的时候吗？举个例子，不要太私人以至于不能在广播里说，所以我请你关注一些对政府监听者来说过于私人、但又不是那么私人以至于你不介意对像两百五十万人说的事。

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:30.340
Right, exactly.
对，没错。

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:33.280
No, nothing like that comes to mind.
不，没有什么那样的浮现脑海。

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:39.380
You know, I find myself being more reserved on the phone than I otherwise might be.
你知道，我发现自己在电话里比平时更加矜持。

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:43.000
Ramzi Qasim has been representing Guantanamo DT&amp;E since 2005.
拉姆齐·卡西姆自2005年以来一直在代理关塔那摩被扣押人员。

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:48.340
He says that his friends and family have definitely commented on his very reserved phone manner.
他说他的朋友和家人绝对评论过他非常保守的通话方式。

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:56.680
You know, everything from, I guess, gentle chiding to, you know, more sort of more pointed annoyance.
你知道，从温和的调侃到，你知道的，某种更尖锐的恼火。

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:05.840
You know, my mother will often, you know, remark in passing that, you know, that I just don't share much.
你知道，我母亲经常会在顺口提到，你知道的，我就是分享得不多。

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:10.160
Well, you know, I have children that are adult children now.
嗯，你知道，我的孩子现在已经是成年人了。

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:11.780
Again, Candace Gorman.
再次是坎迪斯·戈尔曼。

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:22.540
That I worry about some of the things that they're telling me in confidence, things that are going on in their lives that I, you know, don't want the government to know about.
我担心他们秘密告诉我的一些事情，他们生活中正在发生的事情，我，你知道，不想让政府知道的事情。

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:28.620
And I remind them, you know, the most bothersome to me is with the child is far away.
我提醒他们，你知道的，最让我烦恼的是孩子在远方。

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:32.780
So most of our conversations are by text or by phone.
所以我们的大多数对话都是通过短信或电话进行的。

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:37.340
And there's issues that I just think the government has no right to know.
有些问题，我只是认为政府无权知晓。

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:45.900
And is it just like, you know, the personal stuff, which you don't think it would get anybody in trouble or anything, but it's just personal and you just feel like they shouldn't hear that.
这就像是，你知道的，那些私人的东西，你觉得不会给任何人带来麻烦什么的，但它就是私人的，你只是觉得他们不应该听到。

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:48.840
Yeah, that's really what it's about.
对，这正是关键所在。

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:50.020
And it's too personal.
而且这太私人了。

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.920
You know, it's stuff that, you know, you sometimes don't even want to tell good friends of yours.
你知道，有些事情，你知道的，有时你甚至不想告诉你的好朋友。

00:11:56.280 --> 00:12:00.700
Yet the government probably is listening to this some measly little spy.
然而政府可能正在听着，某个微不足道的卑鄙间谍。

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:03.840
Is this conversation being recorded, do you think?
你认为这段对话正在被录音吗？

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:05.220
I believe so.
我相信是的。

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:08.540
And so do you feel self-conscious about this conversation?
那么你对这次谈话会有所戒备吗？

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:10.410
Knowing that it's being recorded?
明知谈话正在被录音？

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:11.600
No, you're just used to it.
不，你只是习惯了。

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:14.590
I'm used to it now, because it's been going on since 2006.
我现在习惯了，因为这种情况从2006年就开始了。

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:18.740
I try to live as though I'm not being monitored.
我尽量表现得像没有受到监视一样生活。

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:22.860
This is David Reams, who has represented two dozen detainees over the years.
这是戴维·里姆斯（David Reams），多年来他曾为二十多名被拘留者辩护。

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:29.060
He told me that when it comes to his cases, he limits what he says on the telephone and Internet to things that it wouldn't bother him to have known.
他告诉我，谈到自己的案件时，他会限制自己在电话和互联网上所说的话，只说那些被别人知道了也无所谓的言论。

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:35.330
But he says that when it comes to monitoring his personal life, it's just like when the government wants to look at his body with an airport scanner.
但他说，至于监视他的个人生活，那就好比政府想用机场扫描仪检查他的身体一样。

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:36.800
He feels like, enjoy yourselves.
他觉得，随便你们怎么看。

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:46.160
In general, I'd say that I have to live my life without thinking all the time about the possibility that I'm being monitored.
总的来说，我想说的是，我必须过好我的生活，而不用时时刻刻去想自己可能正在被监视的这种可能性。

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:52.520
monitored, I simply won't let that concern govern my life.
被监视，我绝不会让这种担忧主宰我的生活。

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:54.300
I can't.
我不能。

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:58.860
I don't see how you can live a normal life if you were always looking over your shoulder.
我不明白如果总是提心吊胆，怎么能过上正常的生活。

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:06.800
I asked all four of these attorneys the thing that I have not been able to figure out all week, namely, should we be upset about these new revelations?
我问了这四位律师一个我一整周都没想明白的问题，也就是说，我们是否应该对这些新曝光的事件感到不安？

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:10.740
After all, we are not being monitored the way that they are, not even close.
毕竟，我们并没有像他们那样受到监视，连边都沾不上。

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:19.700
And we're We're being told safeguards are in place so that no emails are being read, no phones are being tapped of people who are not legitimately part of a real investigation.
而且我们被告知，已经设置了安全保障措施，因此不会有人被读取电子邮件，也不会有人被窃听电话，除非那些人确实是某个真实调查的相关人员。

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:24.380
And remember, there is no proof that anybody has been spied on who should not be.
而且请记住，没有任何证据表明有不该被监视的人受到了监视。

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:27.730
So any abuses still seem very theoretical.
所以任何滥用权力的行为看起来仍然非常理论化。

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:32.540
Like, yes, this could happen in the future, maybe, but nobody has found anything yet.
就像，是的，这在未来可能会发生，也许吧，但目前还没人发现任何问题。

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:34.080
I put all this to Candace Gorman.
我把这一切都抛给了坎迪斯·戈尔曼（Candace Gorman）。

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:37.500
Nothing bad has happened yet, right?
目前还没发生什么坏事，对吧？

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:41.140
Well, that's if you believe your government.
嗯，那是如果你相信你的政府的话。

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:54.500
And, you know, if you believe your government, then back in March of 2013, when Director Clapper was asked, is the NSA collecting any type of data at all on millions or hundreds of millions of people, and he said no.
而且，你知道的，如果你相信你的政府，那么早在2013年3月，当情报总监克拉珀（Clapper）被问及国家安全局（NSA）是否在收集数百万或数亿人的任何类型的数据时，他说没有。

00:13:55.160 --> 00:14:00.000
That was testimony that James Clapper, the Director of National Intelligence, gave in the Senate this March.
那是国家情报总监詹姆斯·克拉珀（James Clapper）今年3月在参议院给出的证词。

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:05.560
So now, of course, it's leaked that, yes, they're collecting information on every single person.
所以现在，当然有泄密称，是的，他们正在收集每个人的信息。

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:10.560
So you have to start from the premise that I do, and that's that the government's not believable.
所以你必须从我的这个前提开始，那就是政府是不可信的。

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:13.940
and they're not going to fess up to anything unless they get caught red-handed.
而且除非他们被抓个现行，否则他们是不会承认任何事情的。

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:25.360
Ramzi Qasim said that it drives him kind of crazy that people, and by people I mean non-lawyers, that he talks to around New York City where he lives seem so undisturbed by the recent revelations.
拉姆齐·卡西姆（Ramzi Qasim）说，人们——他所指的人是他居住的纽约市周围交谈的非律师群体——对最近曝光的事件表现得如此淡定，这让他感到有点抓狂。

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:30.440
He says that we have no idea still what the real guidelines are for whose emails are being read.
他说，对于究竟是谁的电子邮件正在被阅读，我们至今仍然不知道真正的准则是什么。

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:38.960
And when it comes to the phone records that we now know that NSA is getting, which list all of our calls and their duration, he says it's like people don't really get what that means.
而说到我们现在知道国家安全局正在获取的通话记录——其中列出了我们所有的通话及持续时间，他说这就像人们根本不明白那意味着什么。

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:39.180
means?
意味着什么？

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:49.040
You know, I think any one of us, if an FBI agent showed up at our doorstep and basically asked us, you know, who did you call yesterday?
你知道的，我想我们中的任何一个人，如果有个FBI特工出现在我们门前，基本上质问我们，你知道的，你昨天给谁打了电话？

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:49.960
Give me those numbers.
把那些号码给我。

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:57.240
Don't tell me what you said, but give me the numbers that you called, who started the call, how long the call lasted, how many text messages you sent.
别告诉我你说了什么，但把你的通话对象号码、谁发起的通话、通话持续了多长时间、你发送了多少条短信都交出来。

00:14:57.340 --> 00:15:07.100
I don't care about the content of those calls or those text messages, but I just want to know, you know, who you called yesterday and what their numbers are and how long those calls lasted.
我不在乎这些通话或短信的内容，我只想知道，你知道的，你昨天给谁打了电话，他们的号码是什么，以及那些通话持续了多长时间。

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:13.900
I think most of us would be deeply offended by that and we'd be very reluctant to turn over that sort of information.
我想我们大多数人对此都会感到深深的冒犯，并且会非常不情愿交出这类信息。

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:17.680
Maybe.
也许吧。

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:18.960
Yeah, maybe.
对，也许吧。

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:23.260
I heard that and I thought, oh, that's right, that's really creepy, I don't like that.
我听到这话时心想，哦，对了，这真的很瘆人，我不喜欢这样。

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:28.920
And then I played that quote for some other people around the office and they were like, meh, what do I have to hide?
然后我把这段话放给办公室里的其他人听，他们的反应是，呃，我有什么好隐瞒的？

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:38.300
Dayline, Oklahoma.
俄克拉荷马州地标。

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:42.220
This week, crews are still cleaning up in the wake of recent tornadoes around Oklahoma City.
本周，在俄克拉荷马城周边最近遭受龙卷风袭击之后，清理工作仍在继续。

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:43.720
In fact, they've barely begun the cleanup.
事实上，清理工作才刚刚开始。

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:46.420
Just under 4,000 houses and buildings were damaged.
近4000栋房屋和建筑受损。

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:50.060
The first step is actually demolishing the ones that need to come down.
第一步实际上是拆除那些需要被推倒的建筑。

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:55.480
It's going to be months before the debris is cleared and probably another two years before this area is back to where it was before.
要清除废墟还得花上几个月时间，而要让这个地区恢复到以前的面貌，可能还得再等两年。

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:59.780
Annie Correal visited the place that was hit the hardest, the town of Moore.
安妮·科雷尔（Annie Correal）走访了受灾最严重的摩尔镇（Moore）。

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.740
There are literally hundreds of millions to be made off the cleanup in Oklahoma.
在俄克拉荷马州的清理工作中，字面上有数亿美元可赚。

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:05.580
It's a little like a gold rush.
这有点像淘金热。

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:09.020
People have come from all over the country trying to get a piece of it.
人们从全国各地赶来，试图分一杯羹。

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:12.720
The competition among demolition crews in particular is cutthroat.
尤其是拆迁队伍之间的竞争，可以说是极其残酷。

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:14.040
Really cutthroat.
真的是惨烈竞争。

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:22.640
On Saturday, driving around the streets of Moore with a demolition guy named Saul Saltzman, he spots a banner for a competing demolition company on the side of the road.
周六，安妮和名叫索尔·萨尔茨曼（Saul Saltzman）的拆迁队员开车在摩尔镇的街道上转悠时，他发现在路边竖着一块竞争对手拆迁公司的横幅。

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:24.540
Tip-top demolition, huh?
顶级拆迁，哈？

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:27.820
I'm going to tear that sign down later.
我待会儿要把那个牌子给拆了。

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:30.560
I'll have to remember where that is.
我得记住那个地方在哪儿。

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:34.920
Today, Saul's working on getting a job from this big firm.
今天，索尔正忙着从这家大公司接活。

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:38.320
I can't use their name, but let's call them Restoration Company A.
我不能透露他们的名字，但我们叫他们“修复公司A”吧。

00:16:39.380 --> 00:16:44.300
Restoration Company A already has the contract to rip off the roof of a huge damaged warehouse.
修复公司A已经接到了拆除一个严重受损的大型仓库屋顶的合同。

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.880
Saul wants them to hire his crew to do the work.
索尔想让他们雇佣自己的团队来干这活。

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:50.820
So he's laying out his bid to one of the guys in the company.
所以他正在向该公司的一位负责人阐述他的投标方案。

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:54.100
So I'll cover the crane in my bid too.
所以我也会把起重机算进我的出价里。

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:57.460
Crane, torching, I'll scrap it all.
起重机、气割，我全包了。

00:16:57.460 --> 00:16:57.560
all.
全包。

00:16:57.700 --> 00:17:00.080
Well, I'd like to recycle all the metal I can.
嗯，我想回收所有我能回收的金属。

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:05.060
Saul's pretty sure he's going to get the gig, because Restoration Company A owes him one.
索尔非常确信自己能拿下这份工作，因为修复公司A欠他一个人情。

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:12.300
They were in town doing cleanup after the first tornado came through in late May. But then they left, thinking the storms were over.
5月下旬第一次龙卷风过境后，他们就在镇上进行清理。但随后他们以为风暴已经结束，便离开了。

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:14.440
Then another tornado touched down.
接着又有一场龙卷风登陆。

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:16.600
The largest tornado in history.
历史上最大的龙卷风。

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:18.520
2.6 miles wide.
宽度达2.6英里。

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:28.040
And then they called me and said, oh my god, get the semi-truck over there and make it look like like, you know what you're doing, until we get back to town.
然后他们打电话给我说，哦天哪，快把半挂卡车开到那边去，装得好像，你知道自己在干什么一样，直到我们回到镇上。

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:33.180
Saul says he quickly got Restoration Company A's semi and rushed over to set up shop.
索尔说，他迅速弄到了修复公司A的半挂车，并赶过去安营扎寨。

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:34.580
The goal?
目的是什么？

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:40.360
Scare off the competition by making it look like Restoration Company A was already doing the job.
通过营造出修复公司A已经在干活的假象，来吓退竞争对手。

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:46.120
While he was there, Saul says two other Restoration Companies showed up looking to score the contract.
索尔说，当他在那里时，另外两家修复公司也赶到现场，试图抢夺这份合同。

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:47.600
I brought my crew.
我带上了我的团队。

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:49.260
We just pretended we knew what we were doing.
我们只是假装我们知道自己在做什么。

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:54.380
They had their shirts in their trailer, and we put them on, and it was good.
他们的拖车里有他们的工作服，我们穿上了，效果很好。

00:17:55.640 --> 00:18:00.820
Your crew put on the outfits of the restoration company and pretended that you guys were on the job already?
你们的团队穿上了修复公司的制服，然后假装你们已经在干活了？

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:12.020
