yet?
看过吗？

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:18.200
I haven't actually.
其实还没有。

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:39.220
Alright so it's pretty wild because the prosthetic is mostly black with a carbon fiber pattern on the forearm and then this kind of translucent white glove over a black robotic hand and it really looks cyborg.
好吧，这真的很奇妙，因为这个假肢大部分是黑色的，前臂带有碳纤维纹理，然后黑色机器人手上套着一种半透明的白色手套，看起来真的很有赛博朋克的风格。

00:26:40.440 --> 00:27:12.360
You know I'm looking down at my two two hands right now it feels right you know obviously I understand that this is actually not my hand but there must be a part of my brain that says ah yes that's right that's what it's supposed to look like my girlfriend wanted to be here today for this and so tonight I'm sure we're gonna run around the house doing all manner of silly stupid things and making videos yeah it'll probably be one one of the first things that she and I try.
你知道，我现在低头看着我的两只手，感觉很对劲，你知道的，我当然明白这其实不是我的手，但我的大脑里一定有一部分在说：“啊，是的，没错，这才是它该有的样子。”我女朋友今天本想来这里的，所以今晚我敢肯定，我们会满屋子跑，做各种傻里傻气的事情并拍视频，是的，这大概会成为我和她尝试的第一件事情。

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:16.600
Peter Lee, talking to Rio Benin.
彼得·李（Peter Lee）与里奥·贝宁（Rio Benin）交谈。

00:27:24.320 --> 00:27:26.260
Dayline Tuesday, Guantanamo Bay, Cuba.
发稿日期周二，古巴关塔那摩湾。

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:30.660
Hearings resumed on Tuesday in preparation for the trial of Abed Al-Rahim on Nashiri.
周二，审判阿卜杜勒·拉希姆·纳什里（Abed Al-Rahim al-Nashiri）的听证会恢复举行。

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:35.340
Nashiri is accused of organizing the attack on the warship the USS Cole back in 2000.
纳什里被控策划了2000年对美国海军“科尔”号军舰的袭击。

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:37.840
17 sailors were killed in that attack.
那次袭击导致17名水兵丧生。

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:41.380
Nashiri is being tried by one of those Guantanamo military commissions.
纳什里正接受关塔那摩军事委员会之一的审判。

00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:47.020
And just to review the situation at Guantanamo, there are still 166 detainees there.
回顾一下关塔那摩的情况，目前那里仍关押着166名被扣押人员。

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:56.300
At this point, more than half of them, 86, have been cleared for transfer, meaning that the government has concluded that they are not dangerous, but is still holding them.
在这一点上，其中超过一半（即86人）已被批准移交，这意味着政府认定他们并不危险，但仍将他们扣押。

00:27:57.240 --> 00:28:00.140
John Neffel has been covering Guantanamo for Rollingstone.com.
约翰·内费尔（John Neffel）一直为《滚石》杂志网站报道关塔那摩的新闻。

00:28:00.880 --> 00:28:01.680
He's been there twice before.
他之前已经去过那里两次。

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:03.380
He went down again on Monday.
他在周一再次前往。

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:13.280
And it's the first time that John or any reporters have been back to Guantanamo since President Obama gave that speech last month saying he was going to renew efforts to close the prison.
这是自奥巴马总统上个月发表讲话称将重新努力关闭该监狱以来，约翰或任何记者首次重返关塔那摩。

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:19.000
One of the producers of our show, Sarah Koenig, called John to talk about what it was like to be down there this week.
我们节目的制作人之一莎拉·科尼格（Sarah Koenig）打电话给约翰，聊了聊这周在那里的感受。

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:23.680
There are a couple of ways you can watch court proceedings if you're a reporter at Guantanamo.
如果你是关塔那摩的记者，有几种方法可以观看法庭审讯。

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:27.340
You can be in the courtroom, and I use in in air quotes here.
你可以身处法庭之内，我这里的“内”字用了引号。

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:30.940
You sit in a gallery behind three panes of soundproof glass.
你坐在隔音玻璃三层玻璃之后的旁听席上。

00:28:31.660 --> 00:28:35.300
Or you can watch from from inside a media trailer equipped with a closed-circuit feed.
或者你可以从配有闭路电视画面的媒体转播车内部观看。

00:28:36.400 --> 00:28:40.380
Either way, what you're hearing is a 40-second delay of the action in the courtroom.
无论哪种方式，你听到的都是法庭现场画面延迟40秒的音频。

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:50.720
John, who spoke to me from a phone in the media trailer while the Nishiri hearings were underway, says the setup gives you a great appreciation for how very long 40 seconds is.
约翰在纳什里听证会进行期间通过媒体转播车里的电话接受了我的采访，他说这种设置让你对40秒是多么漫长有了深刻的体会。

00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:54.060
Sort of feels like a bad movie dub.
感觉有点像劣质电影的配音。

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:57.730
Can you just describe what you're looking at, like what it looks like?
你能描述一下你看到的情景吗，比如它长什么样？

00:28:57.820 --> 00:28:58.680
Is he in the courtroom?
他在法庭里吗？

00:28:59.780 --> 00:29:02.080
Yeah, he is in the courtroom.
是的，他在法庭里。

00:29:02.080 --> 00:29:02.240
room.
里。

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:07.300
He is wearing white prison issue garb.
他穿着白色的监狱统一囚服。

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:08.860
He has short hair.
他留着短发。

00:29:09.340 --> 00:29:16.820
He's listening to the proceedings on headphones, so it's being translated for him.
他戴着耳机听审判过程，所以有人在为他翻译。

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:19.300
Is the sound just turned down or off?
声音是调小了还是关掉了？

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:22.620
Yeah, I just have the sound down low right now.
对，我现在的声音开得很小。

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:24.660
Do you mind just turning it up so we can hear it?
你介意把它调大一点让我们听听吗？

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:25.260
Is that allowed?
这允许吗？

00:29:26.640 --> 00:30:12.740
No, I think that if I did that, I would be on a plane out of here pretty quickly, unfortunately oh why i mean if it's in the reporter's trailer couldn't why couldn't then it's public no uh the there are signs everywhere that say no recording and um that's uh part of the guidelines oh i see okay right okay yeah that's so yeah and then the other uh particularly particularly interesting thing about the courtroom is that there's a red light next to the judge that either the judge or his assistant, who's called a court security officer, can hit if anyone begins talking about classified information.
不，我想如果我那样做了，恐怕很快就会被用飞机送出这里了。哦，为什么？我的意思是，如果是在记者转播车里，难道……为什么……那不是公开的吗？不，呃，到处都有标牌写着“严禁录音”，嗯，那是……嗯，准则的一部分。哦，我明白了，好吧，对，没错，是的，所以……嗯，然后关于法庭另一个特别有趣的事情是，法官旁边有一个红灯，如果任何人开始谈论机密信息，法官或他的助手（被称为法庭安全官）可以按下这个红灯。

00:30:13.460 --> 00:30:22.140
And so that's the point of this 40-second delay is the stated aim is to protect against accidental disclosures of classified information.
所以，这40秒延迟的目的，官方声称的目的是为了防止意外泄露机密信息。

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:27.760
Does it feel any different from the last time you were there since Obama's speech?
自从奥巴马演讲以来，这里的感觉和上次来有什么不同吗？

00:30:30.180 --> 00:30:34.680
You know, I was sort of thinking that it might, but it really doesn't.
你知道的，我本来以为可能会有，但真的没有。

00:30:34.700 --> 00:30:37.880
I would say that it doesn't feel like anything here has changed.
我要说的是，这里的一切感觉都没有改变。

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:52.320
No, there was testimony in court that there's funding for a fiber optic cable that's going to be laid from here, from the mainland to here to improve internet capabilities.
不，法庭上有证词称，有一笔资金用于铺设一条光缆，将从这里、从美国本土铺设到这里，以改善互联网功能。

00:30:53.300 --> 00:31:01.040
And that's just a sort of small symbol of the inertia to keep Guantanamo open.
这只是保持关塔那摩开放的这种惯性的一个小小的象征。

00:31:01.260 --> 00:31:07.840
And when you're here, it doesn't feel like anyone's moving out at all.
当你身处这里时，你感觉不到有任何人要搬走。

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:15.240
There's no signs of not even its immediate closure, but even that its closure is imminent at some point in the future.
没有任何迹象表明它会立刻关闭，甚至连它在未来某个时候即将关闭的迹象都没有。

00:31:16.200 --> 00:31:19.400
John told me the Nishiri hearings this week were kind of weedy and technical.
约翰告诉我，这周的纳什里听证会有些琐碎且技术性强。

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:27.820
Tuesday, they argued at length about whether defense lawyers were allowed to bring spiral notebooks into the prison, since the wires binding them can be pulled out and used as weapons.
周二，他们进行了长时间的辩驳，争论辩护律师是否被允许将螺旋装订笔记本带入监狱，因为装订它们的铁丝可以被抽出来当做武器使用。

00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:35.080
They also argued about whether the government could use hearsay evidence against Nishiri, something that's rarely allowed in a civilian court.
他们还争论政府是否可以在对纳什里的指控中使用传闻证据，这在民事法庭上是极少被允许的。

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:46.740
And on Friday, they held a closed session, meaning only lawyers and the judge, no press, not even Nishiri himself was allowed to be there, to discuss a motion that is so top secret it doesn't even have a name.
周五，他们举行了一场闭门会议，这意味着只有律师和法官出席，没有媒体，甚至连纳什里本人也不允许到场，以讨论一项机密程度极高、甚至连名字都没有的动议。

00:31:46.750 --> 00:31:51.120
It's referred to as Motion 92 because it's the 92nd court filing in the case.
它被称为第92号动议，因为这是该案件中的第92份法庭文书。

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:56.900
John says he's got this continual feeling when he's down there that he's only ever seeing the tip of the iceberg.
约翰说，当他身处那里时，他有一种持续的感觉，觉得自己永远只能看到冰山一角。

00:31:57.580 --> 00:32:01.920
That with all this monitoring and layers of secrecy, it's impossible to know the whole story of these cases.
由于这一切监视和重重保密，根本不可能了解这些案件的全部真相。

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:04.320
There's so much hidden here.
这里隐藏了太多东西。

00:32:04.980 --> 00:32:07.360
You are covering a trial.
你正在报道一场审判。

00:32:08.000 --> 00:32:15.660
It just happens to be a trial that contains a lot of evidence that no one can tell you.
只不过恰好是一场包含了很多谁也不能告诉你的证据的审判。

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:33.140
I'm wondering, because there's been so much criticism about the form and the structure of these trials and how, you know, they kind of have all the, you know, architecture of real legal proceedings.
我想知道，由于人们对这些审判的形式和结构有这么多的批评，而且你知道，它们拥有真实法律诉讼的所有架构，你知道的。

00:32:33.300 --> 00:32:44.420
But then things happen where, like, you know, maybe somebody at the CIA pulls a plug and suddenly there's no feed or, you know, the defense finds a hidden microphone in the interview cell.
但随后会发生一些事情，比如，你知道的，也许中情局的某个人拔掉了电源，突然间就没有了画面，或者，你知道的，辩护方在会面室内发现了一个隐藏的麦克风。

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:52.680
And so I wonder, like, does it feel all sort of ridiculous, like it's just a show in a kangaroo-courty kind of way?
所以我想知道，这一切感觉是不是都很荒谬，就像是一种袋鼠法庭式的作秀？

00:32:52.800 --> 00:33:02.530
Or does it feel like, no, they're really trying to sort this out and make it legitimate and make it feel as transparent as they can and make it feel legal?
还是说感觉不像那样，他们是真的在努力理清这一切，让它合法化，让它尽可能显得透明，并让它感觉像是合法的？

00:33:02.540 --> 00:33:02.720
goal?
目标？

00:33:04.300 --> 00:33:16.920
I'm kind of of two minds on this, because on the one hand, there are points where it really feels like it's being made up as it goes.
我对此有两种看法，因为一方面，在某些时候，它真的感觉像是走一步算一步、临时拼凑出来的。

00:33:18.360 --> 00:33:25.960
And on the other hand, it does feel like the judge is independent.
而另一方面，它确实让人觉得法官是独立的。

00:33:26.380 --> 00:33:28.770
The defense is adversarial.
辩方是针锋相对的。

00:33:29.540 --> 00:33:36.160
And so it's not exactly as though you're being led down this predetermined path.
所以并不是说你正被牵着走上一条预先决定好的道路。

00:33:36.460 --> 00:33:40.620
It does feel like there can be some amount of deviance.
确实感觉可以有一定程度的偏离。

00:33:41.540 --> 00:33:59.520
But again, the amount of deviance is, I think, within a somewhat narrow structure, especially Especially because, at the end of the day, the government has always contended that there are two authorities for holding people at Guantanamo Bay.
但话又说回来，偏离的程度我认为是在一个相对狭窄的框架之内，特别是考虑到，归根结底，政府一直坚持认为关押在关塔那摩湾的人有两套法律依据。

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:03.760
And in a lot of ways, they don't have that much to do with each other.
而且在很多方面，这两者并没有太大的关联。

00:34:03.900 --> 00:34:11.200
So the one authority is for holding people as war criminals for the violation of war.
因此，其中一个依据是以战争罪犯的身份因违反战争法而关押人员。

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:15.930
So that's what we're seeing in these trials, is whether or not people are guilty of war crimes.
所以我们在这些审判中看到的就是这一点，即人们是否有罪于战争罪。

00:34:15.940 --> 00:34:21.100
But then the other authority is to hold people as prisoners of war.
但另一个依据则是将人员作为战俘进行关押。

00:34:21.600 --> 00:34:32.860
And at the beginning of this week, I asked the lead prosecutor, General Mark Martins, if it was possible that Nishiri could be found not guilty and continued to be held.
在本周初，我问首席检察官马克·马丁斯将军（General Mark Martins），纳什里是否有可能被判无罪但继续被关押。

00:34:33.740 --> 00:34:36.380
And he admitted that, yeah, it is possible.
他承认，是的，这是可能的。

00:34:37.740 --> 00:34:40.520
That hasn't happened, but it's possible.
这还没发生过，但这是可能的。

00:34:41.320 --> 00:35:05.060
And because it's possible, there is a sense that, not that what we're seeing is unimportant, but that the government has a monopoly on all the power, and the outcome of the trial doesn't necessarily mean what it would mean in a civilian court.
正因为这是可能的，所以会给人一种感觉：倒不是说我们所看到的并不重要，而是政府垄断了所有的权力，审判的结果并不一定意味着它在民事法庭上所代表的意义。

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:14.000
Because he's officially there only to cover the Nasheri hearings, John isn't allowed to tour the part of the island where the prisoners are held, and he can't see any sign of them.
由于他官方身份的限制，只被允许报道纳什里的听证会，约翰不能参观岛上关押囚犯的区域，他也看不到关于他们的任何迹象。

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:30.140
Meanwhile, Guantanamo officials say 104 detainees are on hunger strike right now, and 43 of them are being force-fed Ensure or some other nutrition drink, usually twice a day, to keep them alive, though others are taking the Ensure voluntarily under threat of force-feeding.
与此同时，关塔那摩官员表示，目前有104名被扣押人员正在绝食，其中43人被强制鼻饲安素（Ensure）或其他营养饮料，通常一天两次，以维持生命，尽管其他人是在面临强迫喂食的威胁下自愿饮用安素的。

00:35:31.180 --> 00:35:37.420
This hunger strike started in February, And John says lawyers for the detainees tell him their clients aren't despondent or suicidal.
这场绝食抗议始于2月，约翰说，被扣押人员的律师告诉他，他们的当事人并没有沮丧或想要自杀。

00:35:37.820 --> 00:35:43.140
They're protesting, protesting their conditions and the fact that so little is being done to get them out of there.
他们在抗议，抗议他们的生存条件以及当局在营救他们离开这里方面做得太少。

00:35:44.520 --> 00:35:48.680
How much do you know about what the force feeding actually entails physically?
对于强制喂食在生理上究竟包含哪些内容，你了解多少？

00:35:50.660 --> 00:36:17.940
My understanding is that hunger strikers, if they don't go voluntarily, that there's a team that goes in and extracts them and straps them to a chair, and then they're moved into the room where they're force-fed, and then the tube is shoved up their nose and down their throat.
据我了解，绝食抗议者如果不自愿配合，就会有一支队伍进去将他们强行带出并绑在椅子上，然后把他们转移到强制喂食的房间里，接着把管子从鼻孔插进喉咙。

00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:24.400
Lawyers really across the board describe it as an excruciating process.
各方律师普遍将这个过程描述为一种痛苦煎熬的经历。

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:30.940
You were saying that you had spoken to a lawyer of somebody who was being force fed.
你刚才说你曾与一名正被强迫喂食的当事人的律师交谈过。

00:36:31.900 --> 00:36:33.020
Can you talk about that?
能谈谈那件事吗？

00:36:33.580 --> 00:36:34.180
Yeah.
可以。

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:47.380
So one of the detainees, a man named Ahmed Belbaka, who is Algerian, described a nurse who he says is maybe 40 years old.
其中一名被扣押人员，一个名叫艾哈迈德·贝尔巴卡（Ahmed Belbaka）的阿尔及利亚人，描述了一名他认为大约40岁的女护士。

00:36:48.100 --> 00:36:53.840
And he says that she fed him for the first day and her hands were shaking as she did it.
他说，她第一天给他喂食时，手一直在发抖。

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:58.770
So he says, I asked her, is this your first time force-feeding someone?
所以他说：“我问她，这是你第一次强行给别人喂食吗？”

00:36:59.780 --> 00:37:02.420
And the nurse said, yes, it is.
护士说：“是的，我是第一次。”

00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:12.120
And then he, the detainee, told his attorney, quote, some guards tell me I could never take what you are going through.
然后他（被扣押人员）对他的律师说：“有些警卫告诉我，我永远承受不了你正在经历的一切。”

00:37:19.640 --> 00:37:28.640
John says when you're a reporter at Guantanamo, your day is so orderly, so tightly regimented, but it's easy to fall into the comfort of that, to get lost in the day-to-day details.
约翰说，当你在关塔那摩做记者时，你的一天是如此井然有序，受到如此严格的管控，但人很容易陷入那种安逸之中，迷失在日常的琐事里。

00:37:29.020 --> 00:37:33.840
The number of hunger strikers, how many are being force-fed today, what's happening with this or that court motion.
绝食抗议者的数量、今天有多少人正在被强迫喂食、这样或那样的法庭动议进展如何。

00:37:34.580 --> 00:37:36.800
And over time, you start to think it's all kind of normal.
随着时间的推移，你开始觉得这一切都是正常的。

00:37:37.940 --> 00:37:39.770
And he has to remind himself, it's not normal.
而他必须提醒自己，这并不正常。

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:45.400
Every day, he says, quote, I try to remember what an aberration all this is.
他说，他每天都在提醒自己：“我试着记住这一切是多么的异乎寻常。”

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:51.520
Sarah Koenig with John Neffel.
莎拉·科尼格与约翰·内费尔的对话。

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:55.700
He's with Rollingstone.com, and he co-hosts the internet radio show Radio Dispatch.
他供职于《滚石》杂志网站，并共同主持网络广播节目《电台调度》（Radio Dispatch）。

00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:09.060
Dayline Wednesday, lunchtime at a hotel near the Pentagon.
发稿日期周三，五角大楼附近一家酒店的午餐时间。

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:16.360
So if I may ask Mr. Justin, Jason Pittman, excuse me, I was looking at the wrong line.
如果我可以问的话，贾斯汀先生……贾森·皮特曼，抱歉，我看错行了。

00:38:16.400 --> 00:38:20.280
Jason Pittman is our elementary and middle school teacher of the year.
贾森·皮特曼（Jason Pittman）是我们评选出的年度中小学教师。

00:38:20.280 --> 00:38:20.640
Jason.
贾森。

00:38:21.180 --> 00:38:22.200
I'm glad to have you here.
很高兴你能来到这里。

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:28.060
His award was for Aerospace Educator of the Year for the D.W.
他获得的奖项是美国空军协会D.W.

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:30.140
Steele Chapter of the Air Force Association.
斯蒂尔分会颁发的年度航空航天教育家奖。

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:34.080
It was given to a science teacher from Alexandria, Virginia, right near D.C.
该奖项颁给了一位来自弗吉尼亚州亚历山大市（就在华盛顿特区附近）的科学老师。

00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:37.280
This teacher teaches preschool through sixth grade.
这位老师教授从学前班到六年级的课程。

00:38:37.360 --> 00:38:38.520
His name is Jason Pittman.
他的名字叫贾森·皮特曼。

00:38:39.900 --> 00:38:40.960
Congratulations on your award.
祝贺你获奖。

00:38:41.960 --> 00:38:42.580
Thank you very much.
非常感谢。

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:43.100
That was neat.
这太棒了。

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:51.050
It was a really big honor, and I was really honored to get to meet the Chief of Staff of the Air Force, and he said some really touching things to me, so So it was really meaningful.
这真的是一项巨大的荣誉，我非常荣幸能见到空军参谋长，他说了一些让我非常感动的话，所以这真的意义非凡。

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:52.740
And so what did he say?
那么他说了什么？

00:38:54.200 --> 00:39:01.020
You know, he took me aside and he says, he says, thanks for serving your country in this way and thanks for making the choice to teach.
你知道，他把我拉到一边对我说，他说，谢谢你以这种方式为国家服务，也谢谢你选择成为一名教师。

00:39:01.880 --> 00:39:06.500
So he says this to you and you know that this is also your last week of teaching, right?
所以他对你说了这番话，而你也知道这也是你教书的最后一周，对吧？

00:39:07.120 --> 00:39:07.660
That's correct.
完全正确。

00:39:08.440 --> 00:39:09.540
And so what did you say to him?
那你当时对他说什么了？

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:13.280
Well, you know, that gave me a big lump in my throat.
嗯，你知道，这让我感到喉咙哽咽。

00:39:14.440 --> 00:39:16.480
You know, I shook his hand and said, thank you very much.
你知道，我跟他握了手并说，非常感谢。

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:20.400
which I couldn't bring myself to say that this was going to be my last week of teaching.
但我怎么也说不出这其实是我教书的最后一周。

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:29.400
In fact, Friday was his last class after 10 years, winning national awards, one from the National Science Teacher Association, one for leadership and innovation in earth science education.
事实上，周五是他在执教十年后的最后一节课。他曾赢得国家级奖项，其中一个来自全美科学教师协会，另一个是为了表彰他在地球科学教育领域的领导力和创新。

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:32.180
There was a commendation from the Virginia State Senate.
弗吉尼亚州参议院还向他颁发了嘉奖令。

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:34.160
He has his students building robots.
他指导学生制作机器人。

00:39:34.360 --> 00:39:38.320
He was selected to be the teacher on an expedition with Dr. Robert Ballard, the guy who found the Titanic.
他被选中随罗伯特·巴拉德博士（Dr. Robert Ballard）——也就是发现泰坦尼克号的那个人——一起参加探险活动并担任教师。

00:39:39.400 --> 00:39:44.700
Michelle Obama has visited the 13,000 square foot garden where he teaches the science of gardening and health.
米歇尔·奥巴马曾参观过那个13000平方英尺的花园，他在那里教授园艺和健康科学。

00:39:45.260 --> 00:39:45.460
That's right.
没错。

00:39:45.460 --> 00:39:45.960
right.
对。

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:47.700
Yeah, she came to my class and we did lessons together.
是的，她来过我的课堂，我们一起上过课。

00:39:48.300 --> 00:39:51.340
And actually, we've gone and visited the White House quite a few times.
事实上，我们已经去过白宫好几次了。

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:52.340
Okay, right.
好的，没错。

00:39:52.520 --> 00:39:55.160
So if it's going so great, why quit teaching?
既然一切都这么顺利，为什么要辞去教职呢？

00:39:55.820 --> 00:39:56.540
The answer?
答案是什么？

00:39:57.340 --> 00:39:57.820
Money.
钱。

00:39:58.360 --> 00:40:04.080
The funding that pays for his position in the Fairfax County Schools has been rocky for years.
费尔法克斯县学校中支付他薪水的资金多年来一直很不稳定。

00:40:04.940 --> 00:40:11.020
So I work in this program that provides science education for lower-income schools.
我所在的这个项目为低收入学校提供科学教育。

00:40:11.160 --> 00:40:15.600
But that program was cut completely from the county's budget five years ago.
但该项目五年前就被县财政预算完全砍掉了。

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:23.020
Fortunately, the community around the school really wanted him to stay in his job, so they started a non-profit to raise money to keep him in that job.
幸运的是，学校周围的社区非常希望他能留下来，于是他们发起了一个非营利组织来筹集资金，以保住他的工作。

00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:31.120
But what it means is we're trying to get local businesses interested and get folks to fund us to keep this thing in place every year.
但这意味着我们得去吸引当地企业的兴趣，让大家资助我们，以便每年都能维持这个项目。

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:33.480
So really, every year, I don't even know if I have a job.
所以实际上，每年我甚至都不知道自己还有没有工作。

00:40:33.940 --> 00:41:00.880
you know it's it's it's always a lot of legwork you know i've got a i've got to be down at the school board meetings and and um making speeches and wait wait so i understand right so you basically have to raise the money for your own position that's correct yeah i mean it feels like a roller coaster you know like every year we're we're fundraising to kind of to try and and keep this position in place, and it's too many jobs.
你知道的，这总是需要大量的跑腿工作，你知道的，我得去参加学校董事会会议，做演讲，等等，等等——等等，所以我明白了，对吧？也就是说你基本上得为自己的职位筹集资金？完全正确，是的，这感觉就像坐过山车一样，你知道，每年我们都在筹款，试图保留这个职位，而且工作实在太多了。

00:41:02.560 --> 00:41:05.040
You feel like you're doing a good job, and you feel like you have to grovel?
你觉得自己工作表现很好，却又觉得自己像是在卑躬屈膝？

00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:12.540
Is that too strong?
这话会不会说得太重了？

00:41:12.660 --> 00:41:13.340
Is that word too strong?
这个词用得太强了吗？

00:41:13.420 --> 00:41:15.720
No, that's exactly what it feels like.
不，这正是那种感觉。

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:22.500
I mean, you know, like, and we're just, we're not, we are not allowed to say that.
我是说，你知道的，而且我们根本不允许把这种感觉说出口。

00:41:22.500 --> 00:41:22.600
Yeah.
是的。

00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:25.380
We're not allowed to say that we feel like that, you know.
我们不能说我们有这种感觉，你知道的。

00:41:25.520 --> 00:41:26.960
Because you're supposed to be saints or something.
因为你本该是个圣人什么的。

00:41:27.840 --> 00:41:34.400
We're just supposed to be all sacrificing, you know, yeah, you know, ever serving, ever humble.
我们本就该无私奉献，你知道的，是的，你知道的，永远服务，永远谦逊。

00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:39.100
You know, making this move to leave, I feel incredibly selfish.
你知道，做出离开的决定让我觉得自己极其自私。

00:41:39.580 --> 00:41:40.950
You know, teachers aren't supposed to be selfish.
你知道，老师是不该自私的。

00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:44.900
You know, we're supposed to do this job for more than money.
你知道的，我们做这份工作应该不仅仅是为了钱。

00:41:45.880 --> 00:41:48.720
Okay, so maybe this is relevant information about Jason's attitude.
好吧，这或许是了解杰森态度的相关信息。

00:41:48.880 --> 00:41:51.340
Before he did this job, he founded his own tech company.
在从事这份工作之前，他创立了自己的科技公司。

00:41:51.800 --> 00:41:58.220
until he realized that he was always trying to finish his business meetings early so that he could get back to his volunteer teaching job.
直到他发现自己总是试图早点结束商务会议，好赶回他的志愿教师岗位。

00:41:58.650 --> 00:42:01.080
And then he switched careers, went to grad school and switched careers.
然后他转换了职业轨道，去读了研究生并改行。

00:42:01.420 --> 00:42:05.040
He started 10 years ago as a teacher at $57,000 a year.
十年前他刚开始当老师时，年薪是5.7万美元。

00:42:05.160 --> 00:42:09.460
After a few raises and then some school budget cuts, he's still basically making the same thing now.
经过几次加薪以及后来的学校预算削减，他现在的收入基本上还是那么多。

00:42:09.520 --> 00:42:14.300
He's making about $58,000 a year with no real change coming anytime soon.
他现在的年薪大约是58,000美元，而且短期内不会有什么真正的改变。

00:42:14.860 --> 00:42:17.700
He's 38 years old, still paying off his student loans.
他38岁了，还在偿还学生贷款。

00:42:18.540 --> 00:42:22.300
Back in 2008, he bought a townhouse, expecting that his salary was going to rise.
早在2008年，他买了一栋联排别墅，以为自己的薪水会涨。

00:42:22.880 --> 00:42:25.550
It didn't, and so he sold it off in 2011.
结果并没有，于是在2011年他把它卖掉了。

00:42:27.060 --> 00:42:29.860
So my understanding is you don't really want to quit.
所以我的理解是，你其实并不想辞职。

00:42:29.920 --> 00:42:31.060
You're kind of dreading it.
你有点害怕面对它。

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:31.560
Is that right?
是这样吗？

00:42:32.540 --> 00:42:32.880
Yeah.
是的。

00:42:34.340 --> 00:42:35.840
Yeah, I mean, I'm sorry.
是的，我是说，对不起。

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:39.540
It gets me a little choked up.
这让我有点哽咽。

00:42:40.520 --> 00:42:43.640
I want to be here, and I want to do this.
我想留在这里，我想做这份工作。

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:46.260
I want to be here.
我想留在这里。

00:42:46.300 --> 00:42:47.040
I want to do this job.
我想做这份工作。

00:42:48.540 --> 00:42:54.800
But at a point where it just, you know, I want to be able to pay a mortgage and have a car payment.
但在某个阶段，你知道的，我希望能付得起房贷，付得起车贷。

00:42:56.580 --> 00:42:57.860
So it's your last week of teaching.
所以这是你教书的最后一周。

00:42:58.200 --> 00:42:59.280
Like, what's that like?
感觉怎么样？

00:43:01.140 --> 00:43:02.860
It's heartbreaking, actually.
说实话，令人心碎。

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:10.760
It's really, really hard, like, executing some really great lessons and having kids get really, really excited.
真的非常非常难，比如上了一些非常棒的课，然后孩子们表现得非常非常兴奋。

00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:10.940
I did.
我经历过。

00:43:12.200 --> 00:43:17.960
There was a little kid who left my classroom and said, Mr. Pittman, this is the greatest day of my life.
有个小男孩离开教室时说：“皮特曼先生，这是我一生中最棒的一天。我爱科学。”

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:18.580
I love science.
我爱科学。

00:43:19.320 --> 00:43:22.380
And granted, he's only four years old, so I don't have that many days to compete with.
当然，他只有四岁，所以我能与之相比的日子并不多。

00:43:22.880 --> 00:43:42.500
But that was the worst day, to know that I have all these kids that love science, and I know that they love my class, and that they're really getting something something out of it, and I'm going to leave, and I'm really going to miss that.
但那反而是最糟糕的一天，因为我知道有这么多孩子热爱科学，我知道他们喜欢我的课，他们确实从中学到了东西，而我要离开了，我真的会非常怀念这一切。

00:43:44.040 --> 00:43:44.440
Decent pit man.
杰森·皮特曼。

00:43:45.500 --> 00:43:52.800
Knowing that I was going to put this interview on the radio, he told me that he is finally going to tell his principal the news that he's quitting and looking for a new job.
由于知道我要把这次采访放到广播里，他告诉他终于要向校长宣布他要辞职并寻找新工作的消息了。

00:43:53.300 --> 00:44:01.000
I mean, I feel like I've got the skill set, and every year I get a couple of decent offers, so I ought to be able to pick something up pretty quick.
我的意思是，我觉得自己具备这样的技能，而且每年我都会接到几个不错的邀请，所以我应该能很快找到新工作。

00:44:02.480 --> 00:44:07.180
It's horrible, but But if I have to fall back on something, there's always teaching.
这太糟糕了，但如果我真的需要退路，教书永远是一条退路。

00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:12.740
If you have to fall back on something, there's always your dream job.
如果你需要一条退路，你一直都有你梦想的工作。

00:44:13.540 --> 00:44:14.000
Right.
没错。

00:44:15.240 --> 00:44:17.540
In which you're incredibly successful.
而且你在其中取得了巨大的成功。

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:18.770
That'll be your fallback.
那将是你的退路。

00:44:19.660 --> 00:44:20.440
I don't know.
我不知道。

00:44:21.240 --> 00:44:22.980
I'm just having a really tough week.
我只是度过了非常艰难的一周。

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:30.280
Dayline Florida, Wednesday night.
佛罗里达州日间新闻，周三晚上。

00:44:30.280 --> 00:44:30.420
night.
夜晚。

00:44:30.820 --> 00:44:35.040
At 724, a man named William Van Poik was executed by lethal injection.
7点24分，一名叫威廉·范·波伊克（William Van Poyck）的男子被执行注射死刑。

00:44:35.660 --> 00:44:43.880
Van Poik was convicted of killing a prison guard in 1987. Over the years since his trial, two people came forward saying that his accomplice in the crime was the one who pulled the trigger.
范·波伊克于1987年被判谋杀一名狱警罪名成立。在审判后的多年里，有两人站出来说，这起案件中的同伙才是开枪的人。

00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:47.060
Two jurors have said that this would have changed their vote for the death penalty.
两名陪审员表示，这本会改变他们对死刑的投票。

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:51.100
The Florida Supreme Court split twice, four to three on his case.
佛罗里达州最高法院在他的案件上曾两次出现4比3的分裂。

00:44:52.060 --> 00:44:53.540
Van Poik became a writer in prison.
范·波伊克在狱中成了一名作家。

00:44:53.700 --> 00:44:56.820
He wrote three books, two crime novels and a memoir.
他写了三本书，包括两部犯罪小说和一部回忆录。

00:44:57.120 --> 00:44:58.400
He also kept a blog.
他还一直写博客。

00:44:58.520 --> 00:45:03.020
Last month he wrote, when your death warrant gets signed, so many things suddenly become trivial.
上个月他写道：“当你的死刑令签署时，许多事情突然变得微不足道。”

00:45:03.720 --> 00:45:08.640
I've already thrown or given away 95% of my personal property, the stuff that for years seemed so important.
我已经扔掉或送人了95%的个人物品，那些多年来曾显得如此重要的东西。

00:45:09.440 --> 00:45:10.760
All those great books I'll never get to read.
所有那些我再也读不到的好书。

00:45:11.480 --> 00:45:17.160
Reams and reams of legal work I've been dragging around and studying for two decades and which has suddenly lost its relevance.
一叠又一叠我拖了二十年、一直在研究的法律文件，它们突然失去了意义。

00:45:17.920 --> 00:45:25.020
Does it really matter to me now what's happening in the Middle East or on Wall Street or how my Miami dolphins are looking for the upcoming new new season.
中东发生了什么，华尔街怎么样了，或者我的迈阿密海豚队在新赛季表现如何，现在对我和说真的还有关系吗？

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:26.180
What's the point?
有什么意义呢？

00:45:27.300 --> 00:45:31.080
Ditto the TV. The other day, I caught myself reaching for my daily vitamin.
电视也一样。有一天，我发现自己伸手去拿每日维生素。

00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:32.680
Really, I wondered.
真的吗？我在想。

00:45:33.820 --> 00:45:42.540
Now every decision about how to spend the next hour reminds me of Elaine in that Seinfeld episode where she had to constantly evaluate whether her boyfriends were really sponge-worthy.
现在，关于如何度过接下来一个小时的每一个决定，都让我想起《宋飞正传》里伊莲的那集，她不得不不断评估她的男友们是否真的“值得拥有海绵”（译注：指电视剧中的情节，形容挑选标准极其严苛）。

00:45:44.740 --> 00:45:49.920
In Florida, there are now 405 people in death row, and the number that they're actually putting to death is rising.
在佛罗里达州，目前死囚牢房里有405人，而他们实际处决的人数正在上升。

00:45:50.200 --> 00:45:54.080
There was a period of one or two executions a year, or no executions at all.
曾有一段时间，一年只执行一两次死刑，甚至根本没有死刑。

00:45:54.620 --> 00:45:58.900
But in the last few months, Florida Governor Rick Scott has signed six new death warrants.
但在过去几个月里，佛罗里达州长里克·斯科特（Rick Scott）签署了六份新的死刑执行令。

00:45:59.420 --> 00:46:01.740
Van Poyck's execution is the third of those to be carried out.
范·波伊克的死刑是其中被执行的第三起。

00:46:02.020 --> 00:46:06.240
And Emily Bazelon, legal affairs editor at Slate, says they want to go even faster.
《Slate》杂志的法律事务编辑埃米莉·巴泽隆（Emily Bazelon）表示，他们希望把速度变得更快。

00:46:06.760 --> 00:46:13.380
The Florida legislature recently became the first in the country to pass a bill requiring the pace of executions to speed up.
佛罗里达州立法机关最近成为全国第一个通过法案要求加快死刑执行进度的州。

00:46:13.540 --> 00:46:15.280
It's called the Timely Justice Act.
这被称为《及时正义法案》（Timely Justice Act）。

00:46:15.580 --> 00:46:18.960
It sets a deadline of 30 days for the governor to sign a death warrant.
该法案规定了州长签署死刑执行令的30天期限。

00:46:19.940 --> 00:46:21.900
That's after an inmate's appeals become final.
那是在犯人的上诉最终确定之后。

00:46:22.520 --> 00:46:28.340
meaning after at least one round of state and federal appeals and after a review by the governor for clemency.
也就是在至少经过一轮州和联邦上诉，并经州长进行减刑审查之后。

00:46:29.540 --> 00:46:35.420
Once the governor signs the death warrant, the Timely Justice Act says the execution has to occur within 180 days.
一旦州长签署死刑执行令，《及时正义法案》规定必须在180天内执行死刑。

00:46:36.680 --> 00:46:38.260
In Florida, this is a troubling plan.
在佛罗里达州，这是一个令人不安的计划。

00:46:39.280 --> 00:46:39.660
Here is why.
原因如下。

00:46:41.020 --> 00:46:44.320
Florida has the highest rate of error in the country in death penalty cases.
佛罗里达州是全国死刑案件错误率最高的州。

00:46:45.060 --> 00:46:50.050
Since the mid-1970s, it's 77 people executed for 24 exonerated.
自20世纪70年代中期以来，有77人被处决，24人被平反昭雪。

00:46:50.840 --> 00:46:55.740
So in other words, for every three inmates executed, one has been set free.
换句话说，每处决三名囚犯，就有一人被无罪释放。

00:47:03.160 --> 00:47:06.300
Okay, so a couple of questions might have just popped into your head.
好吧，所以你的脑海中可能刚刚蹦出几个问题。

00:47:06.940 --> 00:47:08.560
Why would Florida want to speed up executions?
为什么佛罗里达州想要加快死刑执行？

00:47:09.360 --> 00:47:12.520
And why is the state convicting so many innocent people in the first place?
为什么这个州一开始会判处这么多无辜的人有罪？

00:47:13.500 --> 00:47:14.720
I'm going to start with the second question.
我先从第二个问题开始。

00:47:16.000 --> 00:47:23.000
Florida is the only state in the country where it just takes a simple majority of the jury, a vote of 7 to 5, to send someone to death.
佛罗里达州是全国唯一一个只需要陪审团简单多数票——即7比5的投票结果——就能将某人送上死刑台的州。

00:47:24.100 --> 00:47:28.900
Every other state requires a unanimous jury vote, except for Alabama, where they require 10 votes.
其他所有州都要求陪审团一致投票，阿拉巴马州除外，该州要求10票。

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:34.880
And another huge problem in Florida is the low quality of defense counsel, especially at trial.
佛罗里达州的另一个巨大问题是辩护律师的质量低下，尤其是在审判期间。

00:47:36.020 --> 00:47:47.120
There's a Supreme Court justice in Florida named Raul Cantero who has said that, quote, some of the worst lawyering he has ever seen has been in death penalty cases, where some counsel have, quote, little or no experience.
佛罗里达州有一位最高法院大法官名叫劳尔·坎特罗（Raul Cantero），他曾表示，引述：“他所见过的最糟糕的法律辩护就发生在死刑案件中，一些辩护律师引述‘几乎没有或根本没有经验’。”

00:47:48.840 --> 00:47:58.220
In 2006, the American Bar Association reviewed Florida's whole death penalty system, and they questioned its fairness and accuracy and came up with 11 recommendations for a form.
2006年，美国律师协会审查了佛罗里达州的整个死刑系统，他们对该系统的公正性和准确性提出了质疑，并提出了11项改革建议。

00:47:58.720 --> 00:48:04.780
The Florida Supreme Court and the Florida Bar have also urged comprehensive review, but none of that has happened.
佛罗里达州最高法院和佛罗里达州律师协会也敦促进行全面审查，但这些都没有发生。

00:48:06.260 --> 00:48:07.680
So now let's go back to your first question.
现在让我们回到你的第一个问题。

00:48:08.560 --> 00:48:18.180
Why would Florida look at this set of disturbing exoneration numbers and a disgraceful report from the Bar Association and say, say, hey, I know, let's make this go faster.
为什么佛罗里达州在看到这一系列令人不安的平反数据和来自律师协会的不名誉报告时，会说，嘿，我知道，让我们让这件事情发生得更快吧。

00:48:19.260 --> 00:48:22.360
Especially at a moment when nationally the number of executions is falling.
特别是当全国范围内的死刑执行数量正在下降的时候。

00:48:23.540 --> 00:48:26.620
California and North Carolina haven't executed anyone in years.
加利福尼亚州和北卡罗来纳州已经多年没有处决任何人了。

00:48:27.020 --> 00:48:31.060
In Illinois and Connecticut and in Maryland, they've repealed the death penalty.
伊利诺伊州、康涅狄格州和马里兰州已经废除了死刑。

00:48:31.700 --> 00:48:36.080
And even in Texas, which is the nation's leader in executions, they've been slowing it down.
甚至在死刑执行领跑全国的得克萨斯州，他们也在放慢执行速度。

00:48:37.300 --> 00:48:44.700
I called Florida Representative Matt Gaetz, who's the House sponsor of the Timely Justice Act, to ask him about the bill, but he didn't return my calls.
我打电话给佛罗里达州众议员马特·盖茨（Matt Gaetz），他是《及时正义法案》的众议院提案人，想就该法案询问他，但他没有回电。

00:48:45.140 --> 00:48:51.920
The same thing happened with the state Senate sponsor of the Timely Justice Act, Joe Negron, which I mention only because I think it's lame.
《及时正义法案》的州参议院提案人乔·内格伦（Joe Negron）也是如此，我提到这一点只是因为我认为这太逊了。

00:48:52.840 --> 00:48:57.260
But another state senator who backed the bill, Rob Bradley, agreed to go on tape, which I appreciated.
但另一位支持该法案的州参议员罗布·布拉德利（Rob Bradley）同意接受录音采访，我很感激这一点。

00:48:58.260 --> 00:49:04.480
He's a lawyer, and he says having people sit on death row for 10 or 20 or 30 years, it's just too long.
他是一名律师，他说让人们在死囚牢房里待上10年、20年或30年，时间实在是太长了。

00:49:04.920 --> 00:49:16.480
And so that erodes the public's confidence, and it leaves the impression, rightly or wrongly, that the system is broken and doesn't work and why are we doing this?
这会侵蚀公众的信心，并给人留下一种印象——无论对错——即这个系统已经崩溃，无法运转，我们为什么还要这样做。

00:49:17.380 --> 00:49:24.500
I'd argue that having the highest rate of exonerations in the country might also erode public confidence, which is kind of what I did argue.
我认为，拥有全国最高的无罪释放率可能也会侵蚀公众的信心，这正是我的观点。

00:49:25.380 --> 00:49:30.000
What I'm having trouble with is the fact that Florida has really had such a high error rate.
我感到困惑的是，佛罗里达州居然一直有着如此高的错误率。

00:49:30.300 --> 00:49:37.140
And so I wonder if you're at all nervous that it's possible this new law will make it more likely that the state could execute an an innocent person.
所以我很好奇，你是否丝毫不担心这项新法律可能会让该州处决无辜者的可能性增加？

00:49:38.580 --> 00:49:45.560
This law is not aimed at making it easier to execute someone who is innocent.
这项法律并不是为了更容易地处决无罪之人而设定的。

00:49:46.300 --> 00:49:57.900
Right, but in some cases my understanding is that the idea of having a 30-day period and then a 180-day period is that that will cut off some of the later appeals.
对，但在某些情况下，据我理解，设立30天期限和180天期限的想法会切断一些随后的上诉。

00:49:58.060 --> 00:50:00.180
I just don't see how how that could not be the case.
我只是不明白这怎么可能不是这样。

00:50:00.600 --> 00:50:21.700
What it does is it puts the condemned and his or her lawyers on notice that they need to, if they have claims of innocence, need to gather them and present them to a competent court of law and do so in a timely manner.
它所做的是向死囚及其律师发出通知，如果他们有关于无罪的申诉，他们需要收集这些申诉，并将其提交给有管辖权的法院，并且以及时的方式这样做。

00:50:23.590 --> 00:50:25.760
There are a couple of problems with this argument.
这个论点存在几个问题。

00:50:26.280 --> 00:50:29.480
One is that evidence of innocence can surface at any time.
其一是无罪证据可能在任何时候浮出水面。

00:50:30.340 --> 00:50:32.600
Courts move slowly on these cases for a reason.
法院在这些案件上进展缓慢是有原因的。

00:50:33.300 --> 00:50:36.820
Death is different, as the United States Supreme Court has said many times.
正如美国最高法院多次说过的那样，死刑是不一样的。

00:50:37.880 --> 00:50:39.170
Take the case of Juan Melendez.
以胡安·梅伦德斯（Juan Melendez）的案子为例。他在佛罗里达州的死囚牢房里度过了16年，之后一名辩护调查员在案卷中发现了一段录音。

00:50:39.700 --> 00:50:45.900
He was on Florida's death row for 16 years before a defense investigator discovered a tape in the case files.
他在佛罗里达州的死囚牢房里度过了16年，直到一名辩护调查员在案卷中发现了一盘录音带。

00:50:46.460 --> 00:50:50.800
It was a tape of another man confessing to the murder, and no one had presented it to the jury.
那是另一名男子承认谋杀的录音带，但没有人把它呈交给陪审团。

00:50:51.560 --> 00:50:55.940
Before the tape came out, the Florida Supreme Court rejected his appeals three times.
在这段录音曝光之前，佛罗里达州最高法院曾三次驳回他的上诉。

00:50:56.780 --> 00:51:02.020
If the Timely Justice Act had been in effect at the time, Juan Melendez might easily have been executed.
如果《及时正义法》当时已经生效，胡安·梅嫩德斯很可能早就被处决了。

00:51:02.940 --> 00:51:08.130
I found four more people like that when I looked up the records of all of Florida's 24 exonerees.
当我查阅佛罗里达州全部24名被洗冤者的记录时，我又发现了另外四个这样的人。

00:51:09.660 --> 00:51:12.800
These were men who spent between 13 and 21 years on death row.
这些人都是在死囚牢房里度过了13到21年不等。

00:51:13.780 --> 00:51:17.500
It took time and a lot of work to undo the mistakes that initially doomed them.
纠正那些最初将他们推向绝境的错误，需要时间和大量的工作。

00:51:17.500 --> 00:51:17.660
them.
他们。

00:51:18.760 --> 00:51:21.040
And those mistakes were often made by their own lawyers.
而这些错误往往是由他们自己的律师造成的。

00:51:21.480 --> 00:51:26.160
For example, the trial lawyer for William Van Poik, the man who was executed on Wednesday.
例如，威廉·范·波伊k的审判律师，也就是周三被处决的那名男子。

00:51:27.140 --> 00:51:31.940
That lawyer did no investigation beforehand to give the jury a reason to spare his client's life.
那位律师事先没有做任何调查，以给陪审团一个饶他当事人一命的理由。

00:51:32.960 --> 00:51:40.980
Or Van Poik's lawyer on his first appeal, a cocaine addict who'd previously been disbarred, and never spoke to Van Poik or answered any of his letters.
或者范·波伊k的上诉一审律师，一个曾被取消律师资格的瘾君子，从未与范·波伊k交谈过，也没有回复过他任何信件。

00:51:48.020 --> 00:51:52.800
In theory, the Timely Justice Act would fix this problem by funding defense lawyers.
理论上，《及时正义法》会通过资助辩护律师来解决这个问题。

00:51:53.500 --> 00:52:01.000
But then you read the bill, and you find only about $400,000 to reopen one office for defense lawyers in the northern part of the state.
但当你阅读这项法案时，你发现只有大约40万美元用于重新开放该州北部的一个辩护律师办公室。

00:52:01.940 --> 00:52:04.840
And that office won't handle trials or even the first appeal.
而且那个办公室不负责审理案件，甚至不负责第一次上诉。

00:52:05.100 --> 00:52:07.540
The lawyers will only get involved in the last stage.
律师们只会在最后阶段介入。

00:52:09.360 --> 00:52:13.880
Governor Rick Scott has until next week to decide if he'll sign the Timely Justice Act into law.
州长里克·斯科特要在下周之前决定是否签署《及时正义法》使其成为法律。

00:52:13.880 --> 00:52:14.040
law.
法律。

00:52:14.820 --> 00:52:22.400
If he does, defense lawyers say it will require him to sign 13 new death warrants in the 30 days that follow.
如果他这样做了，辩护律师表示，这将迫使他在随后的30天内签署13份新的死刑执行令。

00:52:25.760 --> 00:52:26.780
Emily Bazelon.
艾米丽·巴泽伦。

00:52:27.180 --> 00:52:28.080
A quick update.
一条简短的最新消息。

00:52:28.280 --> 00:52:35.680
Friday afternoon, after Emily recorded that story that you just heard, Governor Rick Scott announced that he signed the Timely Justice Act into law.
周五下午，在艾米丽录制完你刚刚听到的那个故事后，州长里克·斯科特宣布他已签署《及时正义法》成为法律。

00:52:36.380 --> 00:52:42.540
He said, quote, the bill does not increase the risk of execution of persons who who did not commit murder.
他说，引述如下，该法案并未增加处决未犯谋杀罪之人的风险。

00:52:53.660 --> 00:52:54.380
Deadline, Philadelphia.
截止日期，费城。

00:52:55.320 --> 00:52:56.320
So what happened this week?
那么这周发生了什么？

00:52:57.300 --> 00:53:00.900
This week, we were preparing for the shower for my daughter.
这周，我们一直在为我女儿的婴儿派对做准备。

00:53:01.340 --> 00:53:02.020
She's 22.
她22岁。

00:53:02.260 --> 00:53:03.840
She was having her first baby.
她要生第一个孩子了。

00:53:04.640 --> 00:53:17.320
And it was just fun to be able to share this with my daughter because a year ago I was preparing a funeral, not knowing when she would die, but if she did die, how I would do this.
能够与我的女儿分享这一切真的很有趣，因为一年前我还在准备葬礼，不知道她什么时候会死，但如果她真的死了，我该怎么面对这一切。

00:53:17.420 --> 00:53:20.780
And here I'm now preparing for the birth of my first grandson.
而现在，我正在为我的第一个孙子的出生做准备。

00:53:22.040 --> 00:53:25.740
Last year, Mary Ellen Bowman's daughter, Rachel, was addicted to heroin.
去年，玛丽·艾伦·鲍曼的女儿蕾切尔对海洛因上瘾。

00:53:25.980 --> 00:53:27.160
It was a pretty horrible year.
那是非常糟糕的一年。

00:53:28.060 --> 00:53:29.420
How were you preparing for a funeral?
你当时是怎么准备葬礼的？

00:53:29.600 --> 00:53:30.600
What was going on?
当时到底发生了什么？

00:53:30.920 --> 00:53:33.680
I planned that I was going to have her cremated.
我计划让她火化。

00:53:34.440 --> 00:53:35.780
You mean you started calling around to places?
你是说你开始给各个地方打电话了？

00:53:35.780 --> 00:53:40.840
Yeah, to see what's the procedure, and when I get the ashes, where would I put them?
对，看看流程是什么，当我拿到骨灰时，把它们放在哪里？

00:53:40.900 --> 00:53:45.420
And I found out that where my brother's buried, there was extra room for her to be put there.
我发现我兄弟埋葬的地方有额外空间可以放她。

00:53:46.880 --> 00:53:50.240
What kind of state was she in that you were thinking about her dying?
她当时处于什么状态，让你开始考虑她会死？

00:53:50.440 --> 00:53:51.520
What was she like at home?
她在家里是什么样子的？

00:53:51.940 --> 00:53:53.520
She was very, very thin.
她非常非常瘦。

00:53:54.600 --> 00:53:56.380
She had no veins in her one arm.
她的一只手臂上没有血管。

00:53:56.460 --> 00:54:00.140
They were collapsed, and her eyes were sunk in.
血管都塌陷了，她的眼睛深陷。

00:54:00.660 --> 00:54:04.080
She looked like they watched those stupid zombie movies.
她看起来就像那些愚蠢的僵尸电影里的人。

00:54:04.080 --> 00:54:04.260
Yeah.
对。

00:54:05.180 --> 00:54:06.040
That's what she looked like.
她看起来就是那样。

00:54:06.060 --> 00:54:07.020
I have pictures of it.
我有照片。

00:54:07.400 --> 00:54:08.540
How was she treating you?
她是怎么对待你的？

00:54:08.820 --> 00:54:09.520
Like, what was she like?
比如说，她是个什么样的人？

00:54:09.600 --> 00:54:09.860
Horrible.
太糟糕了。

00:54:11.000 --> 00:54:11.680
Stealing from me.
偷我的东西。

00:54:12.360 --> 00:54:13.220
Cursing at me.
对骂我。

00:54:14.520 --> 00:54:15.040
Abusing me.
虐待我。

00:54:16.460 --> 00:54:17.760
I couldn't trust her.
我无法信任她。

00:54:18.420 --> 00:54:21.720
So it was a vicious year of trying to get help but getting nowhere.
所以这是水深火热的一年，试图寻求帮助却无济于事。

00:54:22.520 --> 00:54:23.860
I finally resorted to praying.
我最后不得不求助于祈祷。

00:54:24.520 --> 00:54:29.530
I know it sounds crazy, but every night at 8 o'clock, I would sit at my sofa.
我知道这听起来很疯狂，但每天晚上8点，我都会坐在沙发上。

00:54:29.740 --> 00:54:32.920
I was praying for the world and anybody addicted to heroin.
我为世界以及任何对海洛因上瘾的人祈祷。

00:54:32.920 --> 00:54:35.500
And then I had a healing prayer for my daughter, Rachel.
然后我为我的女儿蕾切尔做了一个疗愈祈祷。

00:54:36.120 --> 00:54:39.420
And it sort of went briefly, Heavenly Father, you created Rachel.
内容大概是这样的：天父，你创造了蕾切尔。

00:54:39.660 --> 00:54:40.880
You can recreate her.
你能重塑她。

00:54:41.500 --> 00:54:42.560
Please make her free of addiction.
请让她摆脱毒瘾。

00:54:42.880 --> 00:54:43.700
I printed this prayer.
我把这个祈祷文打印了出来。

00:54:43.880 --> 00:54:50.580
I gave it to anybody that would take it, not friends, but people in church, old people, because they have nothing else to do but pray.
我把它给任何愿意接受的人，不是朋友，而是教堂里的人、老年人，因为他们除了祈祷别无他事。

00:54:51.060 --> 00:54:52.360
Usually, you know, they're lonely.
通常，你知道的，他们很孤独。

00:54:52.660 --> 00:54:57.700
And then I went and I also gave it to parents of young kids because I was taught that God hears the prayers of children.
然后我去把这个也给了一些年轻孩子的父母，因为有人教导我说上帝会听取孩子们的祈祷。

00:54:58.800 --> 00:55:25.400
um about nine months ago my daughter came to me and said that uh she wanted me to help her rehab and i witnessed something i had never seen before um a person withdrawing from heroin it's horrible but she did it about the third week she started to have horrible um i'm not vomiting periods in the morning and at night finally i took her to the er and we found that she was pregnant she was I was having morning sickness.
嗯，大约九个月前，我女儿来找我说她想要我帮她去戒毒所，我目睹了我从未见过的情景，嗯，一个人戒海洛因的戒断反应，太可怕了，但她挺过来了，大约在第三周，她开始出现可怕的，嗯，我不是说早晨和晚上都呕吐，最后我带她去了急诊室，我们发现她怀孕了，她当时有孕吐。

00:55:26.440 --> 00:55:27.100
I didn't know it.
我当时并不知道。

00:55:28.840 --> 00:55:37.940
And then, can I just ask you, so on Sunday, during the games and the presents opening, did you find yourself flashing back to a year ago?
然后，我可以问你吗，所以在周日，在玩游戏和拆礼物的时候，你发现自己会闪回一年前的情景吗？

00:55:38.420 --> 00:55:40.500
No, I completely forgot.
没有，我完全忘记了。

00:55:41.080 --> 00:55:47.820
For that day, that moment, to see the difference, it's completely different.
在那一天，那一刻，看到这种差异，完全不同。

00:55:47.960 --> 00:55:52.500
And I cannot believe for that whole day, never once did I think about it.
我简直不敢相信整整一天里，我竟然一次都没有想起过那些。

